requests.text:
{
  "q0": "PhD B noted that the major component in the second waveform was actually the air conditioner, which introduced noise at five hertz. He observed that this noise was not a good illustration of room reverberation but rather of room noise, and that it was inappropriate to use it as an example of reverberation because it was a known, identifiable noise source. He also mentioned that the visual spectrogram differences, particularly in high frequencies, were noticeable and that the noise attenuated the high-frequency components in distant recordings.",
  "q1": "The discussion on Eurospeech covered the upcoming deadline in Denmark in May, with a first deadline on the 13th of May. The group was working on experiments with meeting recorder digits and a special session on Aurora, which had an extended deadline. There was also a note about communication with the OGI gang, where they were exchanging results and working on spectral subtraction. The group acknowledged that the current system showed some degradation in performance, especially on the well-matched case, but there was no significant improvement overall. The idea of using a second stream for voiced-unvoiced detection was discussed as a potential enhancement, though it was noted that spectral subtraction and online normalization do not work well together.",
  "q2": "PhD B observed that the dominant noise in the recordings was from the air conditioner, which was present in the second waveform at five hertz. This noise was not representative of reverberation but rather of room noise, and it was a known, identifiable source. The spectrograms showed that the high frequencies were attenuated in the distant recording, which was a key visual difference. PhD B also noted that the waveforms looked different, but the initial presentation was misleading because it highlighted a second voice rather than reverberation, which was not a clear demonstration of reverberation effects.",
  "q3": "PhD F thought that Eurospeech was a significant opportunity for the group to present results, with a deadline on May 13th. He mentioned that the OGI group was working on integrating spectral subtraction from Ericsson, and they were also exploring their own spectral subtraction methods. He noted that while the current system showed some degradation, especially in the well-matched case, there was no significant improvement. He emphasized the importance of evaluating performance with word error rate rather than accuracy to avoid misjudging the results due to relative improvements in already good systems."
}

