{
  "q0": "The deployed model shows mixed performance: out of six matching conditions, three cases improved, one worsened, and two stayed about the same. Overall, error rates were reduced by approximately a third to a half in different cases, with an average improvement of around 49% on other tasks. However, the best system achieved only about 54% error reduction on the development set, and results were not sufficient for real-world use, especially in noisy or mismatched conditions. The model's performance is still poor, with some cases showing error rates as high as 8–9% per digit, which is unusable for practical applications.",
  "q1": "PhD C asked about experiments using data from multiple languages to train neural nets. PhD A responded that such an approach was tried but did not work well, possibly due to incorrect labeling. They noted that hand-labeled data from other languages is hard to obtain, and while it would be interesting to explore, it is not currently feasible. The discussion concluded that the issue may lie in the data labeling rather than the model design.",
  "q2": "The professor thought the pragmatic overview of the project was valuable because it highlighted the realism of the data used—specifically, real-world noise and acoustic conditions—rather than artificially simulated ones. He emphasized that even with improvements, the results remain poor, indicating that the current methods are not yet sufficient for real applications. He also noted that the project's challenges reflect real-world deployment issues, such as mismatched training and testing conditions (e.g., close vs. distant microphones, different driving conditions), which makes the results more meaningful and realistic.",
  "q3": "The discussion on the pragmatic overview emphasized that the data used in the project reflects real-world conditions—such as real noise, reverberation, and mismatched training/testing environments (e.g., close vs. distant microphones, different driving conditions)—rather than artificial simulations. The professor highlighted that even with improvements, performance remains poor (e.g., 8–9% error per digit), making the results unrealistic for practical use. He viewed this as a strength because it mirrors real deployment scenarios, and the ongoing challenges indicate that significant work remains to make the system viable for real-world applications."
}

